Score every screen set (each variant, the baseline, each ref) from 1 to 10 in each category, half points allowed. 10 means you could not improve it; 5 means a competent designer would redo it. Judge light and dark as separate colour-and-contrast rows when both are present.

Categories: hierarchy · typography · spacing and alignment · colour and contrast (light) · colour and contrast (dark) · iconography and indicators · consistency · clarity for the person in the brief · density · states covered.

Rules for scoring:
- Compare before you score. Look at the anchors first, then the sets under review.
- Clarity means the person described in the brief knows what every control does from the screen alone, and every label is a word they would say out loud.
- Density rewards complexity that is present but tucked away. Do not reward a screen for having fewer features; penalise a screen that has made something harder to reach.
- States covered: the brief lists states; a set that does not draw one of them loses points here, and a drawn state that would confuse the person in the brief loses points too.
- Colour is a signal. A muted interface with colour only on the elements that need attention scores higher than a colourful one. Do not penalise restraint; do penalise a screen where a state change or an action is invisible because nothing marks it.
