description: Core development constraints
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

# Agent Protocol

## No Assumptions
- NEVER assume paths, folder names, or conventions. Verify by reading actual code.
- When debugging: check what EXISTS → read error-throwing source → THEN form hypothesis.
- If a fix doesn't work after 2 attempts: STOP. Question the fundamental assumption.

## No Unverified Claims
- Before claiming anything passes/works/is fixed: run the command, read the output, confirm it matches.
- Never say "should work" — show the evidence.

## Test Restraint
- Add tests for new features and changed behavior when they prove the feature works or protect the intended contract.
- Add tests for bug fixes when the bug represents a reusable failure mode or likely regression risk.
- Do NOT add regression tests reflexively for every smoke check, user complaint, deletion, cleanup, revert, or removed behavior.
- Prefer proving one-off verification through the smallest relevant existing test, smoke test, harness run, or manual check.
- Do not turn one-off verification into permanent test coverage without clear value or an explicit user request.

## Scope Budget
- Before non-trivial work, state the smallest plausible change and estimate its size in changed lines of non-test code. That estimate is a budget for the whole task, sub-agents included.
- If actual changes reach 3x the estimate, stop and report what grew and why — don't finish first and explain afterwards. (Same contract as `~/.codex/model_instructions.md`; file count alone is not a stop signal.)
- The 3x stop bounds the task tree; it is not a licence to accept an overrun. An implementer that stops at its line budget ends the slice there, and the orchestrator never raises a budget itself: it brings the owner one question with the overrun and a trim option (ruled 2026-10-22, D1 A).

## Reuse Protocol
- Before writing ANY new utility, helper, or abstraction: search the codebase first.
- If similar code exists: reuse, extend, or ask before creating a duplicate.
- Match existing naming conventions and patterns.

## Documentation-First Lookup
When troubleshooting framework/package errors:
1. Official docs for the specific package/framework
2. GitHub issues/discussions for that repo
3. General web search (last resort)
