description: LAW (Paul, 2026-10-21) — a PR is capped at 600 added production lines and a review loop at 5 bot rounds; past either, stop and split, never explain
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

# PR Size and Review Rounds — LAW

Ruled by Paul on 2026-10-21 after two PRs in two days reached ~200 review comments each
(wcpos/monorepo#2798, 21 Codex rounds, +2,670 production lines on a 1,200-line spec budget; and a PHP
library PR the day before). "Going through a 200-comment PR is fucking outrageous. Clear sign that
something is deeply, deeply fucking wrong." This binds every model and every agent: Claude sessions,
Codex runs, sub-agents, and anything that opens or pushes to a PR on Paul's behalf.

## The three limits

1. **A PR carries at most 600 added production lines.** Production = everything except tests,
   snapshots, fixtures, docs, lockfiles and generated files. Over 600, the PR is split BEFORE it is
   opened. Size is never explained in a PR body; it is reduced. Enforced by
   `~/.claude/hooks/block-oversized-pr.js` on `gh pr create`.
2. **A spec or stated budget breach is a STOP, not a note.** The 3x scope-budget rule in
   `agent-protocol.mdc` already says this; it also applies when the absolute cap above is nearer than
   3x. Report what grew and propose the split; do not finish first. The budget a brief set is
   enforced too (ruled 2026-10-25, dream D1 A): the orchestrator writes it to
   `~/.claude/state/pr-budget/<branch with / as -->` before dispatching, and `block-oversized-pr.js`
   refuses a PR above it as it refuses 600, until Paul's `/tmp/claude-override-pr-size-<branch>` exists.
   **The brief's budget binds when the PR opens, not after** (ruled 2026-10-31, dream D1 A): once the PR
   is open, review-round fixes and trunk merges grow it under the 600-line law and the round-5 cap only.
   Post-open growth is never a "lift?" question — on 10-30 seven such questions in one day were each
   answered with one word, most for 1–15% growth that review fixes or a trunk merge caused.
   **A ceiling the session sets itself has a floor of 150 added production lines** (ruled 2026-11-02,
   dream D1 A): three sessions in two days briefed 40, 50 and 120 lines on their own and each came back
   with a lift question for 11–28 lines. Paul's own numbers and the 600 law are untouched; the budget
   file is written only for a ceiling he set or one of 150 or more.
3. **A PR's review loop ends at round 5.** Once an open PR carries 15 or more top-level bot review
   threads (Codex, CodeRabbit, Macroscope), no further fix round is pushed. Enforced by
   `~/.claude/hooks/block-review-round-cap.js` on `git push`. The session then (a) writes the
   invariant the findings keep circling in ONE place and deletes the per-branch patches, or (b) splits
   the PR, or (c) brings Paul the open list with a recommendation. A sixth round of branch-by-branch
   fixes is never the answer.

## Why these numbers

- 600 lines is the size one reviewer can hold in their head in one sitting; past it the review becomes
  a serial scan that finds one hole per pass, which is exactly the 21-round pattern.
- 15 threads is round 5 of a reviewer that finds about three things per pass. By then the findings are
  a cluster with one missing invariant, not a tail of independent nits.

## How to split

Ship the mechanical or contract piece first on its own (a protocol bump, a wire-field change, a
renamed field). Ship a new subsystem as a library with its tests and no callers. Then rewire one
caller per PR. Each PR targets the trunk; never stack them (`block-stacked-prs.js`).

## Overrides

Only Paul lifts a limit, per PR, by the file the hook names
(`/tmp/claude-override-pr-size-<branch>` or `/tmp/claude-override-review-cap-<pr>`). His word in chat
is the touch: once he has said "lift" or "go with what you recommend", the session creates the file
and records his words in the budget file (ruled 2026-10-28, dream D1 A). A session never creates the
file unasked and never rewrites a budget ceiling.

What the session recommends depends on what the overrun is made of:

- **Unasked-for code** (locks, fallbacks, abstractions, scope creep) → the **trim or the split, never
  the override** (ruled 2026-10-23, dream D2 A). On 10-22 a session recommended the override twice
  ("four lines of real defect fixes") and he clicked both; a cap whose default pick is "lift it" is a
  button, not a cap.
- **Formatter re-wraps and moved lines** → counted separately and stated with the real number.
- **Ruled scope** — every line is behaviour he approved on a board → the ONE question is "lift to N
  for this PR?" and the recommendation is the lift (ruled 2026-10-28, dream D1 A). On 10-26 a session
  offered to drop a footer line he had approved that morning; *"don't cut a feature just to save 25
  lines of code, what the fuck are you thinking?"* Never name a ruled feature as the thing to cut.

The review-round cap keeps its own rule: the recommendation is the invariant or the split.
