# Mockup

A mockup is a **scored artefact, not a sketch**. Variants are drawn, shot, judged by three models against fixed anchors, polished round by round, and delivered as one static HTML page that Fable picks and opens. Taste calls stay with the main session; the judges rank, they never decide.

**Announce at start:** "Running /mockup — variants, anchors, council rounds."

For a screen that is already built and shot by a journey capture spec (not a mockup), the same loop with the repo's PR rules is in `APP-CLIMB.md`; paste its Block A instead of running the steps below.

## 1. Pin the brief (before any drawing)

**First line of the brief: the mode.** `mode: inside-a-system` when the repo the screen belongs to has design tokens and a component library (a `global.css` of tokens, a `packages/components`, a gallery); `mode: greenfield` otherwise. Inside a system the screen is not a canvas: every element on it already reads a token and a component, and a variant is a change to that system (step 3). Most WCPOS work is inside-a-system.

Write `brief.md` in the output dir with: the mode, the subject (one screen, one job), who stands in front of it, **the problem in the reporter's own words** (Paul's sentence, the ticket's sentence: never the suspected cause, never the category a judge should watch; a brief that says "judge the field affordance" has pointed three judges at fields and away from everything else), the start state, the end state, **every state the screen can be in** (empty, error, long content, loading, offline, permission-denied: whichever apply), the widths to design for, light and dark if the product has both, and the constraints. Constraints come from the repo when it has them: if `.claude/rules/design.mdc` or a `CODING_STANDARDS.md` § Design exists in the current repo, read it and copy the binding lines into the brief. Anything the brief does not pin, pin yourself and mark `assumed:`.

Output dir: the folder Paul named if he named one; else `docs/prototypes/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>/` when the repo has `docs/prototypes/`; else `~/Desktop/mockups/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<slug>/`.

A time or token cap shrinks the round cap (`rounds=1` is the floor: three variants, one judged round, one pick). It never removes variants, anchors or judges. "The process doesn't fit the cap" means set `rounds=1`, not draw one layout and self-assess.

## 2. Freeze the anchors

Shoot once, never change during the climb:
- `anchors/baseline-*.png`: the current screen, if one exists (run the app or capture spec; a headless page is not computer use).
- `anchors/ref-<name>-*.png`: two or three reference screens of the same kind. Sources in order: the Mobbin search tool when this session has it (`search_screens`, download `image_url`), then the competitor notes in the journal vault (`Projects/WooCommerce POS Competitors.md`), then none, stated in the report.
- Inside a system, also `anchors/system-*.png`: the repo's own component gallery (or its gallery shots) and one neighbouring real screen in the same theme, as they are today. Competitors show the task; the system anchors show what every variant must still match. A system mockup with no system anchor has nothing for Consistency to be judged against.

## 3. Draw three variants

Delegate to one Opus agent (the default Agent model; taste ≥ 8). Its prompt carries: `brief.md` in full, `~/.claude/skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md` to read first, the states list as a checklist each variant must render, and the house defaults to avoid by name (cream backgrounds, italic accent words, 01/02/03 section labels, monospace labels, pill buttons, hairline broadsheet rules). Deliverable: `variants/v1.html`, `v2.html`, `v3.html`, each self-contained, CSS only, no JavaScript, each variant a different layout idea, not a recolour. Keep the agent alive; it applies the findings in later rounds.

**Greenfield:** colours as tokens on `:root` with a `:root[data-theme="dark"]` block; the variant invents its system.

**Inside a system, a variant is a diff of the system, and it is written down:**
- `variants/tokens.css` is the repo's live tokens, extracted verbatim (`scripts/tokens-from-css.py <global.css> <out.css>` turns every `@variant <theme> { … }` block into `body[data-theme="<theme>"] { … }`; the theme's shape and weight tokens come with it). Every page loads it and draws each element with the token the component reads in code: open the component and copy its class string, never guess which token a border or a fill uses.
- Each variant carries `variants/vN/changes.md`: every token whose value changes, every element that changes which token it reads, and nothing else. A value or a token that does not exist in the repo today is allowed only as a line there marked `new:`; the same line is repeated verbatim under Decisions I made without you in the final message. No `changes.md`, no variant.
- **Any variant whose `changes.md` touches a token also renders `variants/vN/system.html`: a system sheet.** One row per theme; in the row, every control family that reads the same family of tokens as the change (for an edge or a surface: field, outline button, chip, tab, switch, card, divider; for type: key, value, help, button label, title), each in its resting state, drawn from the same tokens and the same class strings as the subject. The sheet is shot like a variant and handed to the judges as its own set. It is where a change to one element shows what it did to its siblings; the subject screen alone cannot show that, because the subject screen only has the one element on it.

Scope limits what a variant changes; it never licenses what stays to disagree. A sibling control that now reads differently from the changed one (two edge weights, two fills for one role) is inside the scope of every mockup by definition, whatever nouns the request named. The rule about the scope of a ruling (CLAUDE.md) bounds WHICH things change; it is not a reason to drop a finding that an unchanged thing no longer matches.

## 4. Shoot and judge, one round at a time

```bash
bash ~/.claude/skills/mockup/scripts/shoot.sh variants/v1.html rounds/r0/shots   # light+dark, desktop+phone
bash ~/.claude/skills/mockup/scripts/judge-codex.sh rounds/r0                   # writes rounds/r0/codex.md; 3–5 min, run with the Bash tool timeout at 600000
```

Three judges, staged by scarcity, each a **fresh** context every round with `JUDGE.md` verbatim plus the paths of `rounds/rN/shots/`, `anchors/` and (from round 1) `rounds/r0/shots/` as the baseline. Judges never receive the stop condition, a previous round's scores, or another judge's output.
1. **Codex GPT-6.1 Sol** (the script above) alone until every category is 9.0 or above.
2. **Opus** (an Agent on the default model) joins; both must hold 9.0 everywhere.
3. **Fable** (an Agent with `model: "fable"`) judges once, the round after the other two both clear, and its table is the one that confirms the stop. Fable judges only this final round, never earlier.
A judge that has not answered in 20 minutes is scored absent for that round.

After round 0, **pick one variant** from the two score tables and the findings, write the reason in `rounds.md`, and climb only that one. **The pick reads columns, never the average**: a variant with any category under the baseline's, or with a `SYSTEM BREAK` from either judge (JUDGE.md: a sibling on the system sheet that now disagrees with the subject), cannot be picked or climbed; the break is fixed in the system sheet first, or the variant is dropped. Averages break ties between variants that pass, nothing more. A climb never ends with a `SYSTEM BREAK` open: the stop conditions below apply only once both judges report none. Each later round: hand the findings to the variant agent, reshoot, re-judge. Append every table, anchors included, to `rounds.md`.

Stop when all three judges have every category at 9.0 or above (Sol and Opus for two consecutive rounds, Fable once), or two rounds pass with no category rising, or round 5 is reached (`/mockup rounds=N` changes the cap). Revert a round's change for any category that fell before adding anything new.

Taste bounds that outrank any judge: the palette is neutral and colour appears only where it carries meaning; complexity is hidden, never removed (a judge that rewards fewer controls is wrong on that point); nothing the repo's design rules forbid, whatever the score.

## 5. Deliver

`index.html`, static, no JavaScript: the picked variant first at both widths, the states, its system sheet when it has one, the two rejected variants below, the `rounds.md` table, and one before/after pair per change with a one-line label. `open` it. Never hand over a path alone or a hosted artifact link.

Final message, in order: **Decisions I made without you** (every `assumed:` line, every `new:` line from the picked variant's `changes.md` verbatim, and the pick's reason), then the standing four headings. A pick between two variants the judges split on, with the judges' reasons as the options and your pick marked `(Recommended)`, is the only question that goes to Paul.

## Common mistakes

| Mistake | Fix |
|---|---|
| One variant, polished | Three layouts first; the climb starts after the pick |
| Judges told the bar ("get it to 9") | The bar lives here, not in `JUDGE.md` |
| Anchors reshot or swapped mid-climb | Shot once in step 2; their scores drift anyway, read them as relative |
| States missing ("the happy path looks great") | The states checklist is in the brief and is a scored category |
| Delivering files in the scratchpad | Output dir from step 1, `index.html` opened |
| "I rendered it and looked at every shot" as the verdict | Your own look is not a round; the two judges' tables are |
| Skipping the process for a time cap or a named folder | `rounds=1` and the named folder; the shape stays |
| A new token value for the subject, siblings untouched (2026-11-06: a 3.6:1 field edge beside 1.4:1 buttons passed three rounds) | Inside a system every token change renders the system sheet and is judged on it; `SYSTEM BREAK` blocks the pick |
| The brief names the cause or the category to watch | The brief carries the reporter's words, the person and the states; the judges find the cause |
| "Out of scope" used to drop a sibling that no longer matches | Scope bounds what changes, never what must stay coherent |
| Picking on the average while Consistency sits still | Columns first; a category under the baseline disqualifies |
## Subagent dispatch requires multi-agent support

Add to your Codex config (`~/.codex/config.toml`):

```toml
[features]
multi_agent = true
```

This enables the multi-agent tools that skills like
`dispatching-parallel-agents` and `subagent-driven-development` use.
Which tools you get depends on the multi-agent version your model
preset selects (current presets run V2; older ones run V1). Trust your
actual tool list over any table — including this one — when they
disagree.

- **Spawning:** give children a clean context with
  `spawn_agent {fork_turns: "none"}`; the default `"all"` copies your
  entire transcript into the child. On Codex 0.145+, role files under
  `~/.codex/agents/` attach to isolated forks via `agent_type`.
  Full-history forks accept `model` and `reasoning_effort` overrides
  (only `agent_type` is refused there) — isolated forks are the SDD
  default for context hygiene, not because overrides require them.
- **Fix rounds:** resume the implementer with `followup_task` — it
  delivers your message, triggers a turn, and transparently reloads a
  child the harness evicted. Never dispatch a fresh implementer on the
  theory that a spawned agent cannot be messaged again; on V2 it
  always can.
- **Lifecycle:** V2 has no `close_agent`. Finished children are
  evicted automatically when slots are needed; leaving them unclosed
  costs nothing. Only V1 sessions have `close_agent` — there, close
  reviewers when their review returns, and close each implementer
  after its task's review passes.
- **Model names:** never copy a model name from a skill, table, or old
  session into `spawn_agent` without checking it against your current
  spawn allowlist — V2 accepts only V2-capable presets and hard-errors
  on the rest.

## Waiting on children

`wait_agent` is an event subscription, not a poll: a long wait wakes
the moment a child produces mailbox activity, with the same latency as
a short one. Short-timeout polling buys nothing and costs a tool call —
and a context rebill — per poll. In measured sessions, roughly
two-thirds of all wait calls were short polls that timed out.

- While you still have local work, do not wait at all. A completed
  child's final answer is pushed into your mailbox and arrives with
  your next turn.
- When you are genuinely idle with children outstanding, wait in
  bounded stretches: `wait_agent` with `timeout_ms` 300000-600000
  (5-10 minutes). After each stretch — wake or timeout — post one
  status line, run `list_agents`, and chase any child that finished
  without reporting. Never stack polls shorter than five minutes; the
  event subscription wakes a bounded stretch just as fast as a short
  one.
- Completion mail cannot wake an idle controller (it is delivered
  without triggering a turn); covering that idle window is
  `wait_agent`'s only job. A stretch that times out with no activity
  is your cue to reconcile, not to shorten the next stretch.

## Model routing on spawns

Every `spawn_agent` you issue — including when you are yourself a
spawned child running a fan-out — sets `model` AND `reasoning_effort`
explicitly, per the Model Selection rules of the skill you are
executing. Setting `model` alone is a trap: the child's effort
silently resets to that model's default, not to yours.

Ask your human partner to add a machine-level backstop to
`~/.codex/config.toml` so any spawn that slips through still routes to
a deliberate tier instead of silently inheriting the session's most
expensive model:

```toml
[agents]
default_subagent_model = "<a mid-tier model from your spawn allowlist>"
default_subagent_reasoning_effort = "medium"
```

## Environment Detection

Skills that create worktrees or finish branches should detect their
environment with read-only git commands before proceeding:

```bash
GIT_DIR=$(cd "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)
GIT_COMMON=$(cd "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)
BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)
```

- `GIT_DIR != GIT_COMMON` → already in a linked worktree (skip creation)
- `BRANCH` empty → detached HEAD (cannot branch/push/PR from sandbox)

See `using-git-worktrees` Step 0 and `finishing-a-development-branch`
Step 1 for how each skill uses these signals.

## Codex App Finishing

When the sandbox blocks branch/push operations (detached HEAD in an
externally managed worktree), the agent commits all work and informs
the user to use the App's native controls:

- **"Create branch"** — names the branch, then commit/push/PR via App UI
- **"Hand off to local"** — transfers work to the user's local checkout

The agent can still run tests, stage files, and output suggested branch
names, commit messages, and PR descriptions for the user to copy.

